CARD --> GATE
GATE --> META

note right of CARD
  **Why three rows say NOT MEASURED, not a number.**
  backend/eval/run_eval.py's golden sets (golden_sets.py) use
  synthetic deck_id strings like "algorithms_101". LivePipeline
  requires a real lecture UUID and raises rather than silently
  returning zero, so quiz key accuracy, tutor faithfulness and
  synthesis quality cannot be produced against the live system
  with the harness as it exists today -- only backend/eval/
  retrieval_grid.py has a real-corpus golden set, and it only
  computes retrieval metrics. Inventing numbers for the other
  three would be fabrication; leaving them blank states the gap.

  A secondary, incidental finding from the attempt: several
  providers in the text-generation chain (groq, openrouter,
  two gemini models) returned 404 for models that have since
  been deprecated or moved, and cerebras was rate-limited. This
  did not affect the two retrieval rows above (embedding-only,
  a separate call path) but would block a from-scratch synthesis-
  quality run even with a real-corpus golden set.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  23 of 50 questions were skipped by the course-hybrid regime:
  their lecture's course_id is null, so that regime has nothing
  to scope to. This is a real data-completeness gap, not a
  retrieval failure -- it bounds how many of today's lectures
  the course-scoped tutor can actually reach.
end note

note bottom of META
  **Claims this figure can support:** retrieval quality against a
  curated, human-verified golden set of real lecture content,
  measured reproducibly at a known commit, under the exact k and
  threshold the production code uses by default.

  **Claims it can NEVER support, however good the numbers:** that
  students learned more, or anything about quiz/tutor/synthesis
  quality -- those three rows were not run. There is no controlled
  study, no comparison group, and `learning_events` retention
  defaults to 0 (never archive) with no analysis pipeline behind it.
end note

legend bottom
  Full grid (all k x threshold cells, both regimes) in thesis/results/20260917T214528Z/.
  Regenerate with: python -m backend.eval.retrieval_grid --k 3,5,6,8,10 --threshold 0.65
endlegend

@enduml
